## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Analysis

| Original API | Pattern | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` in `Stop()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on cancellation | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue loop after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in `Stop()` | Grace period | `Thread.Join(timeout)` + token |

---

### Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same logic that was in the
                // ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; just continue to let finally run
                // and loop condition will exit naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't exit in time. With cooperative cancellation,
            // this means GetNextItem or ProcessItem isn't respecting the
            // token. Log a warning — you cannot force-kill in .NET 8.
            Console.WriteLine("Warning: worker thread did not exit within timeout.");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

The original `GetNextItem()` may **block indefinitely** (e.g., on a `BlockingCollection` or a socket). You **must** thread the token through:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework) — blocks forever, only Thread.Abort could interrupt
private WorkItem GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks
}

// After (.NET 8) — cancellation-aware
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException
}
```

If it uses `Thread.Sleep`:
```csharp
// Replace Thread.Sleep with cancellation-aware wait
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollInterval);
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `CancellationToken` can't interrupt `Thread.Sleep` or `socket.Receive`. Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or async I/O. |
| **Removing cleanup without relocating it** | The `ThreadAbortException` catch did `RollbackCurrentTransaction()`. This *must* be preserved in the `OperationCanceledException` catch. |
| **Expecting immediate stop** | Cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints. If `ProcessItem` runs for 30 seconds without checking the token, `Stop()` blocks that long. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside long operations. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and registered callbacks. Always implement `IDisposable`. |
| **`volatile bool _running` is now redundant** | The `CancellationToken` replaces it entirely. Remove the field. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort` mental model** | In .NET Framework, `ResetAbort` let the thread survive the abort. In .NET 8, you simply *don't rethrow* — the loop condition `!token.IsCancellationRequested` handles exit. |
| **No force-kill exists in .NET 8** | If code truly can't cooperate (e.g., native call), move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()`. |

---

### If You Need a Timeout-Based Stop

```csharp
public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan timeout)
{
    _cts?.Cancel();
    if (_workerThread is not null && !_workerThread.Join(timeout))
    {
        // Last resort: move uncooperative work to a child process
        throw new TimeoutException("Worker did not respond to cancellation.");
    }
    _cts?.Dispose();
}
```

The fundamental shift: **.NET Framework let you kill threads externally; .NET 8 requires every thread to voluntarily check for cancellation.** Ensure the token is checked at frequent intervals throughout the call chain.
